4.4 How p-values are distributed
A good test of whether we understand p-values is whether we can say how they behave across many studies. Most researchers cannot answer two simple questions: if we ran the same study many times, what would the distribution of p-values look like (a) when the null hypothesis is true, and (b) when the alternative hypothesis is true?

When \(H_0\) is true (and the other assumptions of the null model hold), p-values are uniformly distributed between 0 and 1. Every value is equally likely. This follows directly from the definition: 5% of p-values fall below .05, 1% below .01, and 10% below .10. There is nothing special about .05; it is simply where we have chosen to draw the line.
When \(H_1\) is true, p-values are skewed towards zero, and increasingly so as the true effect size or the sample size increases. The proportion that fall below .05 is, by definition, the study’s statistical power.

Explore this with the plot below. Set the effect size to 0 to see the uniform distribution under \(H_0\), then increase the effect size or sample size and watch the p-values pile up near zero. The dashed line shows what the distribution would be if \(H_0\) were true.

viewof es = Inputs.range([0, 1.5], {value: 0.5, step: 0.05, label: "True effect size (Cohen's d)"})
viewof n = Inputs.range([5, 200], {value: 50, step: 1, label: "Participants per group"})
viewof zoom = Inputs.toggle({label: "Zoom in on p &lt; .10", value: false})























Plot.plot({
  height: 320,
  marginLeft: 50,
  x: {label: "p-value", domain: [0, xmax]},
  y: {label: "Proportion of p-values", grid: true},
  marks: [
    Plot.rectY(bins, {x1: "lo", x2: "hi", y: "prop", fill: d =&gt; d.sig ? "#2780e3" : "#adb5bd", insetLeft: 0.5, insetRight: 0.5}),
    Plot.ruleY([binWidth], {stroke: "currentColor", strokeDasharray: "4,3"}),
    Plot.ruleY([0])
  ]
})







md`Proportion of *p*-values below .05 (${es === 0 ? "the false positive rate, α" : "power"}): **${(100 * pCdf(0.05)).toFixed(1)}%**  
Proportion between .04 and .05: **${(100 * (pCdf(0.05) - pCdf(0.04))).toFixed(1)}%** (if H&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;/sub&gt; were true: 1%)`







delta = es * Math.sqrt(n / 2)
xmax = zoom ? 0.1 : 1
binWidth = zoom ? 0.005 : 0.01
bins = d3.range(Math.round(xmax / binWidth)).map(i =&gt; {
  const lo = i * binWidth, hi = (i + 1) * binWidth
  return {lo, hi, prop: pCdf(hi) - pCdf(lo), sig: hi &lt;= 0.05 + 1e-9}
})
function pCdf(x) {
  return pCdfAt(x, delta)
}
function pCdfAt(x, ncp) {
  if (x &lt;= 0) return 0
  if (x &gt;= 1) return 1
  const z = normInv(1 - x / 2)
  return normCdf(ncp - z) + normCdf(-ncp - z)
}
function normCdf(x) {
  // Abramowitz & Stegun 7.1.26
  const t = 1 / (1 + 0.3275911 * Math.abs(x) / Math.SQRT2)
  const erf = 1 - (((((1.061405429 * t - 1.453152027) * t) + 1.421413741) * t - 0.284496736) * t + 0.254829592) * t * Math.exp(-x * x / 2)
  return x &gt;= 0 ? 0.5 * (1 + erf) : 0.5 * (1 - erf)
}
function normInv(p) {
  // Acklam's algorithm
  const a = [-3.969683028665376e+01, 2.209460984245205e+02, -2.759285104469687e+02, 1.383577518672690e+02, -3.066479806614716e+01, 2.506628277459239e+00]
  const b = [-5.447609879822406e+01, 1.615858368580409e+02, -1.556989798598866e+02, 6.680131188771972e+01, -1.328068155288572e+01]
  const c = [-7.784894002430293e-03, -3.223964580411365e-01, -2.400758277161838e+00, -2.549732539343734e+00, 4.374664141464968e+00, 2.938163982698783e+00]
  const e = [7.784695709041462e-03, 3.224671290700398e-01, 2.445134137142996e+00, 3.754408661907416e+00]
  const plow = 0.02425
  if (p &lt; plow) {
    const q = Math.sqrt(-2 * Math.log(p))
    return (((((c[0] * q + c[1]) * q + c[2]) * q + c[3]) * q + c[4]) * q + c[5]) / ((((e[0] * q + e[1]) * q + e[2]) * q + e[3]) * q + 1)
  }
  if (p &gt; 1 - plow) return -normInv(1 - p)
  const q = p - 0.5, r = q * q
  return (((((a[0] * r + a[1]) * r + a[2]) * r + a[3]) * r + a[4]) * r + a[5]) * q / (((((b[0] * r + b[1]) * r + b[2]) * r + b[3]) * r + b[4]) * r + 1)
}

























































For a fuller version, including the distribution of p-values for any power and the option to simulate studies one at a time, see Kristoffer Magnusson’s interactive visualisation at rpsychologist.com/d3/pdist.

4.4.0.1 The dance of the p-values
The distribution in the plot above is also what we would see if we repeated the same study many times: same design, same sample size, same true effect. Each repetition gives a different p-value, and the spread is much wider than most researchers expect. With 50% power, an exact replication of a study could easily produce anything from p &lt; .001 to p &gt; .50. Cumming (2008) called this the “dance of the p-values”. In one simulation of 25 repetitions of a typical experiment, p ranged from below .001 to .76. He showed that if an initial experiment gives a two-tailed p of .05, there is an 80% chance that the one-tailed p-value from an exact replication will fall anywhere between .00008 and .44, and that this interval is just as wide whatever the sample size.
The plot below lets you watch the dance. Each new study is an exact replication with the same true effect and sample size. The left panel shows each study’s estimated effect size and its 95% confidence interval; the right panel shows its p-value. Notice how much the p-values jump around, even though nothing about the study is changing, and how the confidence intervals convey the same uncertainty much more honestly. By default the true effect is zero, so the p-values are spread evenly between 0 and 1, and every significant result is a false positive. Then try a true effect of d = 0.5, which with 32 participants per group gives about 50% power: the p-values shift towards zero, but still dance across almost the whole range.

viewof danceEs = Inputs.range([0, 1.5], {value: 0, step: 0.05, label: "True effect size (Cohen's d)"})
viewof danceN = Inputs.range([5, 200], {value: 32, step: 1, label: "Participants per group"})
viewof danceAuto = Inputs.toggle({label: "Run automatically", value: true})
viewof danceOne = Inputs.button("Run one more study")






























html`&lt;div style="display: flex; gap: 10px;"&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align: center; font-weight: 600;"&gt;Cohen's &lt;i&gt;d&lt;/i&gt; effect size&lt;/div&gt;${danceLeft}&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align: center; font-weight: 600;"&gt;&lt;i&gt;p&lt;/i&gt;-value&lt;/div&gt;${danceRight}&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;`







md`Studies run: **${danceStudies.length}**. Significant: **${danceStudies.filter(d =&gt; d.sig).length}** (${danceStudies.length ? (100 * danceStudies.filter(d =&gt; d.sig).length / danceStudies.length).toFixed(0) : 0}%), compared with ${(100 * pCdfAt(0.05, danceEs / danceSe)).toFixed(0)}% expected ${danceEs === 0 ? "from the false positive rate (α), because the true effect is zero" : "from the study's power"}. Changing the settings starts again.`







// Simulated exact replications. Each study's observed effect is drawn from its sampling distribution,
// d_obs ~ Normal(d, se) with se = sqrt(2 / n), and tested with the same normal approximation used in the plots above.
mutable danceStudies = []







danceSe = Math.sqrt(2 / danceN)
danceShown = danceStudies.slice(-20)
danceLast = danceStudies.length ? danceStudies.at(-1).i : 0
danceXDomain = [Math.min(-0.5, danceEs - 4 * danceSe), Math.max(1, danceEs + 4 * danceSe)]
danceYDomain = [Math.max(0.5, danceLast - 19.5), Math.max(20.5, danceLast + 0.5)]
danceColour = d =&gt; d.sig ? "#2780e3" : "#adb5bd"
danceLeft = Plot.plot({
  width: Math.max(160, Math.floor((width - 10) / 2)), height: 360, marginLeft: 15, marginRight: 15, clip: true,
  x: {label: "Effect size (d) and 95% CI", domain: danceXDomain, grid: true},
  y: {label: "Study", domain: danceYDomain, reverse: true, ticks: []},
  marks: [
    Plot.frame({stroke: "currentColor"}),
    Plot.ruleX([0], {stroke: "currentColor"}),
    Plot.ruleX([danceEs], {stroke: "currentColor", strokeDasharray: "4,3"}),
    Plot.ruleY(danceShown, {y: "i", x1: "lo", x2: "hi", stroke: danceColour, strokeWidth: 2}),
    Plot.dot(danceShown, {x: "d", y: "i", fill: danceColour, r: 4})
  ]
})
danceRight = Plot.plot({
  width: Math.max(160, Math.floor((width - 10) / 2)), height: 360, marginLeft: 15, marginRight: 15, clip: true,
  x: {label: "p-value", domain: [0, 1], grid: true, ticks: [0, 0.05, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1], tickFormat: d =&gt; d === 0 ? "0" : String(d).replace(/^0/, "")},
  y: {label: "Study", domain: danceYDomain, reverse: true, ticks: []},
  marks: [
    Plot.frame({stroke: "currentColor"}),
    Plot.ruleX([0.05], {stroke: "currentColor", strokeDasharray: "4,3"}),
    Plot.line(danceShown, {x: "p", y: "i", stroke: "#adb5bd", strokeOpacity: 0.6}),
    Plot.dot(danceShown, {x: "p", y: "i", fill: danceColour, r: 4})
  ]
})


























































danceRunner = {
  const se = Math.sqrt(2 / danceN)
  const draw = d3.randomNormal(danceEs, se)
  let i = 0
  mutable danceStudies = []
  const addStudy = () =&gt; {
    const d = draw()
    const p = 2 * (1 - normCdf(Math.abs(d / se)))
    i += 1
    mutable danceStudies = [...mutable danceStudies, {i, d, lo: d - 1.96 * se, hi: d + 1.96 * se, p, sig: p &lt; 0.05}]
  }
  const onClick = () =&gt; addStudy()
  viewof danceOne.addEventListener("input", onClick)
  invalidation.then(() =&gt; viewof danceOne.removeEventListener("input", onClick))
  addStudy()
  while (true) {
    yield Promises.delay(900, i)
    if (viewof danceAuto.value) addStudy()
  }
}






A single p-value, in other words, tells us surprisingly little about what the next study will find. This is the replication delusion (Section 4.3.3) seen from the other side. And the dance happens even here, where every assumption of the null model other than \(H_0\) holds by construction (Section 4.2.1.1). A surprising p-value on its own therefore cannot distinguish chance from a failed assumption, which is why, for trustworthiness assessment, patterns across many results matter more than any single value.
For a fuller version, Kristoffer Magnusson’s simulation of p-values lets you draw samples from a population one at a time and see how much the p-value jumps around from sample to sample, as well as the effects of p-hacking and publication bias on the results.


4.4.0.2 Optional stopping: the dance within a single study
The studies in the plot above were independent replications. The p-value also dances within a single study as data accumulate, and this creates a particularly seductive form of p-hacking: optional stopping, or checking the results as participants are added and stopping data collection once p &lt; .05. This was one of the practices Simmons et al. (2011) showed could inflate false positive rates, and its effects have been known since at least Armitage et al. (1969).
In the simulation below, a single study starts with 5 participants per group (a total N of 10) and adds one more participant to each group at a time, up to a total N of 100. After each new pair of participants, the researcher runs a t-test and stops as soon as p falls below .05. By default there is no true effect.

viewof seqEs = Inputs.range([0, 1], {value: 0, step: 0.05, label: "True effect size (Cohen's d)"})
viewof seqContinue = Inputs.button("Continue data collection")
viewof seqReset = Inputs.button("Reset")























html`&lt;div style="display: flex; gap: 10px;"&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align: center; font-weight: 600;"&gt;Cohen's &lt;i&gt;d&lt;/i&gt; effect size (95% CI)&lt;/div&gt;${seqLeft}&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align: center; font-weight: 600;"&gt;&lt;i&gt;p&lt;/i&gt;-value&lt;/div&gt;${seqRight}&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;`







{
  const last = seqData.path.at(-1)
  if (!last) return md``
  const fmt = x =&gt; x &lt; 0.001 ? "&lt; .001" : "= " + x.toFixed(3).replace(/^0/, "")
  const fmtD = x =&gt; x.toFixed(2)
  const stop = seqData.path.find(d =&gt; d.n === seqData.stopN)
  if (seqData.mode === "running") return md`Collecting data: *N* = ${last.N}, *d* = ${fmtD(last.d)}, *p* ${fmt(last.p)}.`
  if (seqData.mode === "stopped") return md`**Stopped at *N* = ${stop.N}, *d* = ${fmtD(stop.d)}, *p* ${fmt(stop.p)}.** A researcher who checks after every new pair of participants and stops as soon as *p* &lt; .05 would stop here and report a significant result. Press "Continue data collection" to see what would have happened next.`
  if (seqData.mode === "never") return md`Reached the maximum of *N* = ${last.N} (*d* = ${fmtD(last.d)}, *p* ${fmt(last.p)}) without *p* ever falling below .05. Press "Reset" to run a new study.`
  return md`Stopped at *N* = ${stop.N} (*d* = ${fmtD(stop.d)}, *p* ${fmt(stop.p)}), but kept collecting data: now *N* = ${last.N}, *d* = ${fmtD(last.d)}, *p* ${fmt(last.p)}.`
}







// State of the simulated sequential study: the path of estimates and p-values, where it stopped, and its mode
// ("running", "stopped", "continuing", "never", "finished").
mutable seqData = ({path: [], stopN: null, mode: "running"})







seqNMax = 100  // total N; the study runs to seqNMax / 2 per group
seqColour = d =&gt; d.sig ? "#2780e3" : "#adb5bd"
seqStop = seqData.path.filter(d =&gt; d.n === seqData.stopN)
seqLeft = Plot.plot({
  width: Math.max(160, Math.floor((width - 10) / 2)), height: 320, marginLeft: 35, marginRight: 15, clip: true,
  x: {label: "Total N", domain: [0, seqNMax], grid: true},
  y: {label: null, domain: [Math.min(-1.5, seqEs - 1.5), Math.max(1.5, seqEs + 1.5)], grid: true},
  marks: [
    Plot.frame({stroke: "currentColor"}),
    Plot.areaY(seqData.path, {x: "N", y1: "lo", y2: "hi", fill: "#2780e3", fillOpacity: 0.15}),
    Plot.ruleY([0], {stroke: "currentColor"}),
    Plot.ruleY([seqEs], {stroke: "currentColor", strokeDasharray: "4,3"}),
    Plot.line(seqData.path, {x: "N", y: "d", stroke: "#2780e3"}),
    Plot.ruleX(seqData.stopN ? [2 * seqData.stopN] : [], {stroke: "#2780e3", strokeDasharray: "2,2"})
  ]
})
seqRight = Plot.plot({
  width: Math.max(160, Math.floor((width - 10) / 2)), height: 320, marginLeft: 35, marginRight: 15, clip: true,
  x: {label: "Total N", domain: [0, seqNMax], grid: true},
  y: {label: null, domain: [0, 1], grid: true, ticks: [0, 0.05, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1], tickFormat: d =&gt; d === 0 ? "0" : String(d).replace(/^0/, "")},
  marks: [
    Plot.frame({stroke: "currentColor"}),
    Plot.ruleY([0.05], {stroke: "currentColor", strokeDasharray: "4,3"}),
    Plot.line(seqData.path, {x: "N", y: "p", stroke: "#adb5bd"}),
    Plot.dot(seqData.path.filter(d =&gt; d.sig), {x: "N", y: "p", fill: "#2780e3", r: 2}),
    Plot.ruleX(seqData.stopN ? [2 * seqData.stopN] : [], {stroke: "#2780e3", strokeDasharray: "2,2"}),
    Plot.dot(seqStop, {x: "N", y: "p", stroke: "#2780e3", r: 6})
  ]
})





































// A single sequential study: n0 participants per group, then `step` more per group at a time, with an independent-samples
// t-test (pooled variance) after each addition. Uses the exact t distribution rather than the normal approximation
// used above, because the normal approximation overstates significance at very small n. Stops at the first p &lt; .05
// unless continued. The 95% CI for d uses the large-sample approximation se = sqrt(2 / n).
seqRunner = {
  const n0 = 5, step = 1
  const drawA = d3.randomNormal(seqEs, 1), drawB = d3.randomNormal(0, 1)
  let s1, s2, ss1, ss2, n, path, stopN, mode
  const publish = () =&gt; { mutable seqData = ({path: [...path], stopN, mode}) }
  const addBatch = k =&gt; {
    for (let j = 0; j &lt; k; j++) {
      const a = drawA(), b = drawB()
      s1 += a; ss1 += a * a; s2 += b; ss2 += b * b
    }
    n += k
    const m1 = s1 / n, m2 = s2 / n
    const sp = Math.sqrt(((ss1 - n * m1 * m1) + (ss2 - n * m2 * m2)) / (2 * (n - 1)))
    const d = (m1 - m2) / sp, se = Math.sqrt(2 / n)
    const t = (m1 - m2) / (sp * Math.sqrt(2 / n)), df = 2 * n - 2
    const p = tTwoSidedP(t, df)
    path.push({n, N: 2 * n, d, lo: d - 1.96 * se, hi: d + 1.96 * se, p, sig: p &lt; 0.05})
    if (mode === "running" && p &lt; 0.05) { mode = "stopped"; stopN = n }
    if (2 * n &gt;= seqNMax && (mode === "running" || mode === "continuing")) mode = mode === "running" ? "never" : "finished"
  }
  const reset = () =&gt; {
    s1 = s2 = ss1 = ss2 = 0; n = 0; path = []; stopN = null; mode = "running"
    addBatch(n0); publish()
  }
  const onContinue = () =&gt; { if (mode === "stopped") { mode = "continuing"; publish() } }
  viewof seqContinue.addEventListener("input", onContinue)
  viewof seqReset.addEventListener("input", reset)
  invalidation.then(() =&gt; {
    viewof seqContinue.removeEventListener("input", onContinue)
    viewof seqReset.removeEventListener("input", reset)
  })
  reset()
  while (true) {
    yield Promises.delay(60, n)
    if ((mode === "running" || mode === "continuing") && 2 * n &lt; seqNMax) { addBatch(step); publish() }
  }
}







function tTwoSidedP(t, df) {
  const x = df / (df + t * t)
  return incBeta(x, df / 2, 0.5)
}
function incBeta(x, a, b) {
  if (x &lt;= 0) return 0
  if (x &gt;= 1) return 1
  const lbeta = logGamma(a + b) - logGamma(a) - logGamma(b) + a * Math.log(x) + b * Math.log(1 - x)
  if (x &lt; (a + 1) / (a + b + 2)) return Math.exp(lbeta) * betaCf(x, a, b) / a
  return 1 - Math.exp(lbeta) * betaCf(1 - x, b, a) / b
}
function betaCf(x, a, b) {
  const tiny = 1e-30
  let c = 1, d = 1 - (a + b) * x / (a + 1)
  d = Math.abs(d) &lt; tiny ? tiny : d
  d = 1 / d
  let h = d
  for (let m = 1; m &lt;= 200; m++) {
    const m2 = 2 * m
    let aa = m * (b - m) * x / ((a + m2 - 1) * (a + m2))
    d = 1 + aa * d; d = Math.abs(d) &lt; tiny ? tiny : d
    c = 1 + aa / c; c = Math.abs(c) &lt; tiny ? tiny : c
    d = 1 / d; h *= d * c
    aa = -(a + m) * (a + b + m) * x / ((a + m2) * (a + m2 + 1))
    d = 1 + aa * d; d = Math.abs(d) &lt; tiny ? tiny : d
    c = 1 + aa / c; c = Math.abs(c) &lt; tiny ? tiny : c
    d = 1 / d
    const del = d * c
    h *= del
    if (Math.abs(del - 1) &lt; 1e-12) break
  }
  return h
}
function logGamma(z) {
  // Lanczos approximation
  const g = [676.5203681218851, -1259.1392167224028, 771.32342877765313, -176.61502916214059,
             12.507343278686905, -0.13857109526572012, 9.9843695780195716e-6, 1.5056327351493116e-7]
  if (z &lt; 0.5) return Math.log(Math.PI / Math.sin(Math.PI * z)) - logGamma(1 - z)
  z -= 1
  let x = 0.99999999999980993
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; 8; i++) x += g[i] / (z + i + 1)
  const t = z + 7.5
  return 0.5 * Math.log(2 * Math.PI) + (z + 0.5) * Math.log(t) - t + Math.log(x)
}





























Run it a few times. Often the p-value never falls below .05. But with no true effect, simulations show that about 28% of studies run this way cross .05 at some point before reaching a total N of 100, compared with the 5% false positive rate that a single test at a fixed sample size would give.
Watch the path of the p-value in the runs that do stop. It often drifts steadily downwards over many additions before crossing .05, which looks like an effect gradually emerging as the sample grows. From inside the study, stopping at that point feels like the natural conclusion of a trend: the evidence was accumulating, and it finally became clear. But that impression is created by the stopping rule itself. Because data collection ends at the first point below .05, the researcher only ever sees the part of the path that ends in significance, never what would have come next. Press “Continue data collection” and, when there is no true effect, the p-value usually wanders back up: in simulations with these settings, about 70% of the later checks in studies that would have stopped were nonsignificant, and only about 5% of studies were significant at the full sample size, exactly as expected for a single test. The “trend” was a random walk, cut off at a convenient moment.
Notice also the effect size at the stopping point. Because the study stops at the moment chance has pushed the estimate far enough from zero to be significant, the reported effect size is typically inflated, often far above the true value, and it shrinks back towards the truth as data collection continues. This is the same mechanism that makes significant results overestimate effects in general (Section 4.3.3), concentrated into a single study.
The effect size panel tells the same story more honestly. As data accumulate, the confidence interval narrows steadily around the true effect, regardless of where the p-value happens to be. With a true effect (try d = 0.3), the p-value does head towards zero and stay there as the sample grows, because the null model really is false. The problem with optional stopping is that, with the stopping rule in place, the two situations can produce results that look the same.
This does not mean that looking at data as they accumulate is illegitimate. Sequential designs that plan the number of looks in advance and adjust the significance threshold at each one keep the false positive rate at its nominal level, and can be more efficient than fixed designs (Lakens, 2014). The problem is unplanned and undisclosed peeking, which violates the null model’s assumption that the analysis was planned before seeing the data (Section 4.2.1.1).


4.4.1 Barely significant p-values
p-values between about .02 and .05 count as statistically significant (when using the default \(\alpha\) = .05), and they are very common in the published literature. But if we take the distribution of p-values seriously, they are surprisingly rare in well-powered studies of true effects. Bogdan (2025) referred to these as ‘fragile’ p-values. Use the calculator in Table 4.2 to see what proportion of significant results should be fragile, or strong, for a given level of power. It starts with a well-powered study (95% power), where only about 5% of significant results fall between .02 and .05; try lowering the power to see how quickly this changes. Then try raising the power to 99%: a fragile p-value then becomes more probable under \(H_0\) than under \(H_1\) (Lakens, 2015, 2022).

viewof calcPower = Inputs.range([5, 99], {value: 95, step: 1, label: "Statistical power (%)"})
viewof calcAlpha = Inputs.range([0.01, 0.1], {value: 0.05, step: 0.01, label: "Significance threshold (α)"})
viewof calcFragile = Inputs.range([0.01, 0.1], {value: 0.02, step: 0.01, label: "Lower bound of 'fragile' range"})
viewof calcStrong = Inputs.range([0.005, 0.05], {value: 0.005, step: 0.005, label: "Upper bound of 'strong' range"})
































Table 4.2: Expected share of significant p-values (two-sided) that are fragile or strong, if the alternative hypothesis is true with the chosen power, or if the null hypothesis is true. Calculated with the normal approximation used in the plot above. The final column compares how likely a p-value in each range is under \(H_1\) and under \(H_0\), and converts this into the probability of the more probable hypothesis. This assumes both hypotheses were equally probable before seeing the data (a 50:50 prior); with a different prior the answer changes (see Section 4.3.4).



calcValid
  ? html`&lt;table class="table table-sm" style="width: auto; margin: 0 auto;"&gt;
      &lt;thead&gt;&lt;tr&gt;
        &lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
        &lt;th style="text-align: right;"&gt;% of significant results in range if H&lt;sub&gt;1&lt;/sub&gt; is true&lt;/th&gt;
        &lt;th style="text-align: right;"&gt;% of significant results in range if H&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;/sub&gt; is true&lt;/th&gt;
        &lt;th style="text-align: right;"&gt;More probable hypothesis&lt;/th&gt;
      &lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/thead&gt;
      &lt;tbody&gt;
        &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style="text-align: left;"&gt;Fragile: ${fmtP(calcFragile)} ≤ &lt;i&gt;p&lt;/i&gt; &lt; ${fmtP(calcAlpha)}&lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td style="text-align: right;"&gt;${fmtPct(fragileH1)}&lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td style="text-align: right;"&gt;${fmtPct(fragileH0)}&lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td style="text-align: right;"&gt;${morePlausible(fragileLR)}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
        &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style="text-align: left;"&gt;Strong: &lt;i&gt;p&lt;/i&gt; ≤ ${fmtP(calcStrong)}&lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td style="text-align: right;"&gt;${fmtPct(strongH1)}&lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td style="text-align: right;"&gt;${fmtPct(strongH0)}&lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td style="text-align: right;"&gt;${morePlausible(strongLR)}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
      &lt;/tbody&gt;
    &lt;/table&gt;`
  : html`&lt;p style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;i&gt;The bounds of the fragile and strong ranges must both be below α.&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/p&gt;`










calcValid = calcFragile &lt; calcAlpha && calcStrong &lt; calcAlpha
calcNcp = normInv(1 - calcAlpha / 2) + normInv(calcPower / 100)
calcSig = pCdfAt(calcAlpha, calcNcp)
fragileH1 = (calcSig - pCdfAt(calcFragile, calcNcp)) / calcSig
strongH1 = pCdfAt(calcStrong, calcNcp) / calcSig
fragileH0 = (calcAlpha - calcFragile) / calcAlpha
strongH0 = calcStrong / calcAlpha
// Likelihood ratio of a p-value falling in each range under H1 vs H0 (unconditional probabilities, not shares of
// significant results), converted to the posterior probability of the more probable hypothesis given a 50:50 prior.
fragileLR = (calcSig - pCdfAt(calcFragile, calcNcp)) / (calcAlpha - calcFragile)
strongLR = pCdfAt(calcStrong, calcNcp) / calcStrong
function morePlausible(lr) {
  const pH1 = lr / (1 + lr)
  return pH1 &gt;= 0.5
    ? html`H&lt;sub&gt;1&lt;/sub&gt; (${(100 * pH1).toFixed(0)}%)`
    : html`H&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;/sub&gt; (${(100 * (1 - pH1)).toFixed(0)}%)`
}
fmtP = x =&gt; String(+x.toFixed(4)).replace(/^0/, "")
fmtPct = x =&gt; (100 * x).toFixed(x &lt; 0.1 ? 1 : 0) + "%"





















































































In a well-powered study of a real effect, most significant p-values are very small. Barely significant p-values are the typical outcome of low power, or of a true null hypothesis, where they make up 60% of significant results. At very high power, a p-value between .04 and .05 is actually more likely when \(H_0\) is true than when \(H_1\) is true (Lakens, 2015, 2022).
Both sides of a well-known debate about significance thresholds accept this. Benjamin et al. (2018), in proposing to “redefine statistical significance” from .05 to .005, pointed out that a two-sided p of .05 corresponds to a Bayes factor of only about 2.5 to 3.4 in favour of \(H_1\), which is weak evidence, and that in replication projects the replication rate was roughly twice as high for original studies with p &lt; .005 as for those with .005 &lt; p &lt; .05 (50% vs. 24% in psychology). Lakens et al. (2018), replying that researchers should instead “justify your alpha”, agreed that replication rates were lower for p-values just below .05, but argued that this difference is likely confounded by p-hacking rather than reflecting the evidential value of those p-values alone.
The replication evidence is consistent across projects. In the Reproducibility Project: Psychology (Open Science Collaboration, 2015), 63% (20 of 32) of original studies with p &lt; .001 produced a significant result when replicated, compared with 41% (26 of 63) with p &lt; .02, 26% (6 of 23) with .02 &lt; p &lt; .04, and 18% (2 of 11) with p &gt; .04. Similarly, across 103 replications in large-scale replication projects, Gordon et al. (2021) found that 74% of original findings with p ≤ .005 replicated, compared with 28% of those with p &gt; .005. At the level of whole articles, Bogdan (2025) found that the percentage of an article’s significant p-values that were fragile predicted whether its findings replicated, across 113 replication attempts.
Despite this, barely significant results are over-represented in the published literature. Leggett et al. (2013) found that “just significant” results had become more common over time. In the largest analysis to date, Bogdan (2025) extracted p-values from 240,355 empirical psychology articles published between 2004 and 2024 and calculated the percentage of each article’s significant p-values that were “fragile” (.01 ≤ p &lt; .05). Before the replication crisis (2004–2011), 32% of significant p-values were fragile, well above the 26% expected if studies had 80% power. This has since fallen to about 26%, in line with increasing sample sizes, although many individual articles still report mostly fragile results. More broadly, published literatures contain far more significant results than their statistical power could produce. Scheel et al. (2021) found that 96% of a random sample of standard psychology articles reported support for their first hypothesis, compared with 44% of Registered Reports, where the decision to publish is made before the results are known. Within individual multi-study articles, the same problem can be tested directly: if each of four studies has 50% power, the probability that all four produce a significant result is only 6%; even at 80% power it is 41% (Lakens & Etz, 2017; Schimmack, 2012). Using this logic, known as the test of excess significance (Ioannidis & Trikalinos, 2007), Francis (2014) found that 82% of articles with four or more experiments published in Psychological Science between 2009 and 2012 reported more significant results than their power could plausibly produce, and Francis et al. (2014) found the same for 83% of qualifying psychology articles in Science.
The underlying message was put memorably by Rosnow and Rosenthal (1989, p. 1277):

“Surely, God loves the .06 nearly as much as the .05.”
Rosnow and Rosenthal (1989)

They meant that .05 is an arbitrary line and that p = .06 should not be dismissed where p &lt; .05 is accepted as meaningful evidence. However, the distribution of p-values suggests a less comforting corollary: in a well-powered study of a true effect, neither .05 nor .06 is a very likely outcome. Perhaps God loves neither very much.


4.4.2 Marginally significant p-values
The mirror image of the barely significant result is the marginally significant one: a p-value just above .05 that is described as if it were nearly significant, for example as “approaching significance” or “a trend towards significance”. This allows authors to have it both ways, acknowledging that the result did not meet the threshold while still treating it as support for their hypothesis.
The practice is common. Olsson-Collentine et al. (2019) extracted 42,504 p-values between .05 and .10 from 44,200 articles in APA journals published between 1985 and 2016, and found that almost 40% were described as marginally significant, ranging from 30% in clinical psychology to 45% in organisational psychology. Pritschet et al. (2016) examined over 1,500 articles in top cognitive, developmental, and social psychology journals between 1970 and 2010. In 1970, 18% of articles described at least one p-value as marginally significant; by 2000 it was 52%, and in social psychology a majority of articles did so in both 2000 (59%) and 2010 (54%). Although most of these p-values fell between .05 and .10, values as large as .18 were sometimes described as marginally significant. The larger analysis by Olsson-Collentine et al. (2019) did not find this increasing trend from 1985 onwards, but both studies agree that the practice is common. Otte et al. (2022) searched 567,758 randomised controlled trials published between 1990 and 2020 and found phrases describing “almost significant” results in 8.65% of them; the most common were “marginally significant”, “all but significant”, “a nonsignificant trend”, “failed to reach statistical significance”, and “a strong trend”.
The phrases Otte et al. searched for came from a list of over 500 compiled from the published literature by Matthew Hankins under the title Still not significant. The list is worth reading in full, both as a catalogue of euphemisms to watch for and as a funny read. Examples range from “a borderline significant trend (p = .09)” to “a clear tendency to significance (p = .052)”.
The evidential problem is the same as for barely significant results, only worse. At 80% power, a p-value between .05 and .10 is only about 1.5 times as likely under \(H_1\) as under \(H_0\), and at 95% power it is less likely under \(H_1\) than under \(H_0\). Olsson-Collentine et al. (2019) accordingly recommended against describing such results as marginally significant. Benjamin et al. (2018) went further, proposing that even results with .005 &lt; p &lt; .05 should be called “suggestive” rather than significant.


4.4.3 Bias: p-value distributions that cannot occur naturally
Because unbiased p-values are either flat (under \(H_0\)) or skewed towards zero (under \(H_1\)), there is no natural way for a collection of p-values to cluster just below .05. In null-model terms (Section 4.2.1.1), this is what the distribution looks like when the analysis and reporting assumptions fail: if the analysis was chosen, or the result selected, because of its p-value, the reference distribution no longer applies and p-values are no longer uniform even when \(H_0\) is true. And yet, reported p-values in the literature often look like this. In their seminal article, Masicampo and Lalande (2012) reported such a “peculiar prevalence” of p-values just below .05 in three psychology journals. In a much larger sample of 258,050 p-values from eight psychology journals, Hartgerink et al. (2016) also found a bump just below .05 among exactly reported p-values, in three of the journals. However, the bump largely disappeared when p-values were recalculated from the reported test statistics, suggesting that much of it arises in how p-values are reported rather than in the underlying results.
Publication bias alone cannot produce this pattern: withholding nonsignificant results thins the distribution above .05, but cannot create a pile-up just below it (Hartgerink et al., 2016). That requires results to be moved across the threshold, whether through p-hacking, misreporting, or fabrication. Hartgerink et al.’s data point to misreporting as one contributor. Of the p-values reported as exactly .05, 67% were larger than .05 when recalculated from their test statistics, and 14% were larger than .055, so could not have been legitimately rounded to .05. Such downward rounding was more common for p = .05 than for p = .04, .03, or .02 (50–54%), suggesting that it is concentrated at the threshold for significance. They described this as direct evidence of the questionable research practice of “incorrect rounding of p-values” (John et al., 2012). The same asymmetry appears in reporting errors more generally: Nuijten et al. (2016) found that grossly inconsistent p-values (where the reported p-value and the one implied by the test statistic fall on opposite sides of .05) were more common among p-values reported as significant (1.56%) than among those reported as nonsignificant (0.97%), which they suggested could indicate a systematic bias in favour of significant results. Whether any individual case is careless or strategic cannot be determined from the numbers alone. But no conventional rounding rule turns .058 into .05.
Figure 4.1 illustrates what this kind of distortion looks like. It shows a hypothetical literature in which half of the tested hypotheses are true and studies have 50% power. The dashed line shows the distribution of p-values we would expect without bias. The bars show what happens if 30% of the results that would have fallen between .05 and .10 are instead nudged to just below .05, for example by trying a different outlier exclusion rule or adding a covariate: a dip above the threshold and a bump just below it.

