// Illustrative biased literature: 50% true nulls (uniform p-values) and 50% true effects studied with 50% power
// (noncentrality 1.96). 30% of the p-values in (.05, .10] are moved to (.04, .05], two-thirds of them to (.045, .05].
biasedBins = {
  const w = 0.005, ncpHalfPower = 1.96, moved = 0.3
  const b = d3.range(20).map(i =&gt; {
    const lo = i * w, hi = (i + 1) * w
    const unbiased = 0.5 * w + 0.5 * (pCdfAt(hi, ncpHalfPower) - pCdfAt(lo, ncpHalfPower))
    return {lo, hi, unbiased, biased: unbiased, sig: hi &lt;= 0.05 + 1e-9}
  })
  const removed = d3.sum(b.slice(10), d =&gt; d.unbiased * moved)
  b.slice(10).forEach(d =&gt; d.biased = d.unbiased * (1 - moved))
  b[8].biased += removed / 3
  b[9].biased += removed * 2 / 3
  return b
}






Similar distortions are not limited to p-values. Wherever a rule-of-thumb threshold determines whether a result is considered acceptable, there is pressure to meet it. Hussey et al. (2025) found a similar excess of Cronbach’s α values at the conventional .70 threshold for acceptable reliability, across more than 180,000 α values from the general psychology literature, the industrial and organisational psychology literature, and the APA’s PsycTests database.
It is important to be clear about the scope of this for trustworthiness assessment. p-hacking and publication bias are serious problems for the field, and their signatures are visible when many p-values are pooled across a literature. But they are very difficult to establish for any individual article: a single study that was p-hacked and one that was not can report exactly the same results, and a missing file drawer leaves no trace in the articles that were published. For this reason, detecting p-hacking and publication bias is not covered directly in this book as part of trustworthiness assessment. The methods in later chapters focus instead on what can be checked within an article, such as whether its reported results are possible and internally consistent.
Influential studies on sources of bias in p-values:

p-hacking. Simmons et al. (2011), “False-positive psychology”, is the seminal text on p-hacking. Using simulations and a deliberately absurd example study, it showed how a handful of common and individually defensible analytic choices (collecting more data after checking the results, choosing among dependent variables, adding covariates, dropping conditions) can push the false positive rate far above the nominal 5%. Stefan and Schönbrodt (2023) compiled a compendium of 12 p-hacking strategies from the literature and simulated the impact of each on false positive rates. Their accompanying interactive web app lets you explore how each strategy affects false positive rates and the distribution of p-values.
Publication bias. Sterling (1959) was the first to document that almost all articles in psychology journals using significance tests reported significant results. Rosenthal (1979) named the resulting “file drawer problem”: if nonsignificant results are more likely to stay in researchers’ file drawers, the published literature can be made up disproportionately of the Type I errors, and meta-analyses of the published literature will tend to overestimate effects. The file drawer effect is almost universally recognised, but it was not until relatively recently that it was directly estimated: Franco et al. (2014) were able to observe the file drawer directly, by following a complete set of preregistered survey experiments and showing that studies with null results were far less likely to be written up and published than those with strong results.



4.4.4 Detecting biased p-values
The shape of a set of significant p-values is also the basis of methods that aim to diagnose a given literature: p-curve, which tests for “evidential value” and p-hacking and estimates effect sizes corrected for publication bias (Simonsohn et al., 2014a, 2014b), and Z-curve, which estimates the expected replication and discovery rates of a set of studies (Bartoš & Schimmack, 2022; Brunner & Schimmack, 2020). Both methods are widely used, and both have been seriously criticised.

p-curve. Several forms of p-hacking do not produce the left-skewed distribution that p-curve looks for. Ulrich and Miller (2015) showed this for selecting among multiple analyses, prompting a revised version of the method (Simonsohn et al., 2015). Bishop and Thompson (2016) showed it for selectively reporting among uncorrelated outcome variables. Conversely, a right-skewed p-curve does not guarantee true effects: in observational research, even minimal confounding makes true effects indistinguishable from p-hacked null effects (Bruns & Ioannidis, 2016), and a left-skewed curve can arise from selective non-reporting even when all effects are real (Erdfelder & Heck, 2019). Its effect size estimates are biased under moderate to large heterogeneity (McShane et al., 2016; van Aert et al., 2016). Most recently, Morey and Davis-Stober (2026) showed that the p-curve tests lack basic properties expected of statistical tests, and recommended against using them.
Z-curve. Pek et al. (2026) argued that z-curve’s expected discovery rate has no clear link to credibility, that its estimators can be biased and inconsistent, and that most published applications do not check its assumptions (e.g., independent p-values from focal tests). They recommended against using it to evaluate research findings. Schimmack and Soto (2026) replied that these limitations apply to selection models in general, not to z-curve specifically.

Given this unresolved debate and, more importantly, the fact that these methods can only be applied to a large number of papers (e.g., a literature on a given effect), these methods are not covered further in this book, which focuses on methods for detecting impossible or implausible results within individual articles.

5.1 Means and Standard Deviations
Standardised effect sizes are built from means and standard deviations, so it helps to have a feel for what each of them describes. The plot below shows a single sample as a normal distribution. Use the sliders to change its mean and its SD. The mean sets where the distribution sits, and is marked by the dashed line. The SD sets how spread out the distribution is: the bar marks the scores within one SD of the mean.
viewof oneMean = Inputs.range([1, 7], {value: 4, step: 0.05, label: "Mean"})
viewof oneSd = Inputs.range([0.5, 2], {value: 1, step: 0.05, label: "SD"})
{
  // one normal distribution
  const density = x =&gt; Math.exp(-(((x - oneMean) / oneSd) ** 2) / 2) / (oneSd * Math.sqrt(2 * Math.PI))
  // both axes are fixed, so that the distribution changes when the sliders move and the axes do not
  const xMin = -4, xMax = 12, yMax = 1.15 / (0.5 * Math.sqrt(2 * Math.PI))
  const peak = density(oneMean), shoulder = density(oneMean + oneSd)
  const curve = d3.range(xMin, xMax + 0.001, 0.05).map(x =&gt; ({x, y: density(x)}))
  const blue = "#2780e3"
  return Plot.plot({
    width, height: Math.min(360, Math.max(260, width * 0.5)), marginLeft: 20, marginRight: 20, marginTop: 10, marginBottom: 45,
    x: {label: "Score", labelAnchor: "center", labelOffset: 38, domain: [xMin, xMax], ticks: d3.range(-4, 13, 2)},
    y: {axis: null, domain: [0, yMax]},
    style: {fontSize: "13px"},
    marks: [
      Plot.areaY(curve, {x: "x", y1: 0, y2: "y", fill: blue, fillOpacity: 0.2}),
      Plot.lineY(curve, {x: "x", y: "y", stroke: blue, strokeWidth: 2}),
      Plot.ruleY([0], {stroke: "currentColor"}),
      // the mean
      Plot.link([{}], {x1: oneMean, x2: oneMean, y1: 0, y2: peak, stroke: blue, strokeDasharray: "4,3"}),
      Plot.text([{}], {x: oneMean, y: peak, text: () =&gt; `M = ${oneMean.toFixed(2)}`, dy: -10, fontWeight: 600}),
      // one SD either side of the mean, drawn where the curve is one SD from the mean
      Plot.link([{}], {x1: oneMean - oneSd, x2: oneMean + oneSd, y1: shoulder, y2: shoulder, stroke: "currentColor", strokeWidth: 1.5}),
      Plot.text([{}], {x: oneMean + oneSd, y: shoulder, text: () =&gt; `SD = ${oneSd.toFixed(2)}`, textAnchor: "start", dx: 8, fontWeight: 600})
    ]
  })
}
A normal distribution has no minimum or maximum, but most scales in psychology do: a 1–7 Likert item cannot produce a response of 0 or of 8. Set the mean to 6 and the SD to 2, and a large part of the distribution lies above 7. A sample on a 1–7 scale could not look like this. On a bounded scale, the closer the mean is to either end, the smaller the largest possible SD becomes, which is taken up in Section 8.1.

5.2 Standardized Mean Differences (Cohen’s d)
Cohen’s d for two independent groups is the difference between their means divided by their pooled standard deviation:
\[
d = \frac{M_1 - M_2}{SD_{pooled}}, \qquad SD_{pooled} = \sqrt{\frac{(n_1 - 1)SD_1^2 + (n_2 - 1)SD_2^2}{n_1 + n_2 - 2}}
\]
Because Cohen’s d is a difference between two means in SD units, it can be pictured as the distance between two distributions relative to how wide they are. The plot below shows a control condition and an intervention condition as two normal distributions with the same SD. The three sliders are linked by d = mean difference / SD. Change Cohen’s d and the mean difference changes to match; change the mean difference or the SD and Cohen’s d changes to match. See how far apart the two distributions are, and how much they overlap, for effect sizes of different magnitudes, and notice that the same d can come from a small difference between narrow distributions or a large difference between wide ones. Two other ways of expressing the same effect size are shown under the plot: the percentage of the two distributions that overlaps, and the probability of superiority, which is the probability that a randomly chosen participant from the intervention condition scores higher than a randomly chosen participant from the control condition. Both assume normal distributions with equal SDs. This plot is a simplified version of the interactive visualisation by Kristoffer Magnusson at rpsychologist.com/cohend.

viewof dLinked = {
  // three linked sliders: Cohen's d = mean difference / SD. changing d changes the mean difference, and changing
  // the mean difference or the SD changes d. the value, {d, difference, sd}, is stored on the wrapping div
  const dMax = 3, sdMax = 2
  const round = x =&gt; Math.round(x * 100) / 100
  const dInput = Inputs.range([0, dMax], {value: 0.5, step: 0.01, label: "Cohen's d"})
  const differenceInput = Inputs.range([0, dMax * sdMax], {value: 0.5, step: 0.01, label: "Mean difference"})
  const sdInput = Inputs.range([0.5, sdMax], {value: 1, step: 0.01, label: "SD (both conditions)"})
  const view = html`&lt;div&gt;${dInput}${differenceInput}${sdInput}&lt;/div&gt;`
  const store = () =&gt; { view.value = {d: dInput.value, difference: differenceInput.value, sd: sdInput.value} }
  // each slider's own listener runs before its input event reaches the wrapping div, so the other sliders are
  // already up to date when the plot reads the value
  dInput.addEventListener("input", () =&gt; { differenceInput.value = round(dInput.value * sdInput.value); store() })
  const updateD = () =&gt; {
    // the mean difference cannot be more than the largest d on the slider allows for this SD
    if (differenceInput.value &gt; dMax * sdInput.value) differenceInput.value = round(dMax * sdInput.value)
    dInput.value = round(differenceInput.value / sdInput.value)
    store()
  }
  differenceInput.addEventListener("input", updateD)
  sdInput.addEventListener("input", updateD)
  store()
  return view
}







