Several of the default examples are worth a closer look. F(1, 7) = 12.19, p &lt; .001 is one of the results that Schmidt (2017) reported was flagged in his own article. It is inconsistent, because its p-value is .010, but it does not change the conclusion. r(40) = −.30, p = .03 is grossly inconsistent, whereas r(40) = .3, p = .03, one of the examples in the plot in Section 10.1.1.3, is consistent: the second decimal narrows the range of possible p-values enough to exclude .03. And the last example, which has an MSE between the test statistic and the p-value, is not recognised at all.


10.1.1.2 How common are inconsistent p-values?
Nuijten et al. (2016) used statcheck to check every article published in eight major psychology journals between 1985 and 2013: 30,717 articles in total. statcheck found APA-style results in 16,695 of them (54.4%), and these articles reported 258,105 p-values.

49.6%
of articles (8,273 of 16,695) contained at least one inconsistent p-value.


12.9%
of articles (2,150) contained at least one grossly inconsistent p-value.


9.7%
of all p-values (24,961) were inconsistent.


1.4%
of all p-values (3,581) were grossly inconsistent.

That is, around half of psychology articles that report significance tests contain at least one p-value that does not match its test statistic, and around one in eight contain one where the mismatch changes whether the result is significant.
The inconsistencies were also not symmetric. Of all p-values reported as significant, 1.56% were grossly inconsistent, compared with 0.97% of those reported as non-significant.

1.6×
as likely to be grossly inconsistent when a p-value was reported as significant as when it was reported as non-significant.

This is what we would expect if errors that produce significant results are more likely to be made, less likely to be caught, or more likely to be published. Nuijten et al. (2016) discuss all three possibilities, and their data cannot distinguish between them.
These figures are estimates, and depend on what statcheck can read. statcheck only reads results reported in full APA style in the text. When Nuijten et al. (2016) compared statcheck with a manual check of 49 articles, statcheck found 67.5% of the results that had been extracted by hand. Most of the results it missed were in tables, or had an effect size or other statistic between the test statistic and the p-value (e.g., “F(2, 70) = 4.48, MSE = 6.61, p &lt; .02”). How common inconsistencies are among the results that statcheck does not read is unknown.


10.1.1.3 Rounding: statcheck checks an interval, not a value
A reported test statistic is a rounded number. When an article reports t(28) = 2.0, the t that was calculated from the data could have been anything from 1.95 to 2.05, all of which round to 2.0. These values of t give two-sided p-values from .0613 down to .0498. A reported p &lt; .05 is therefore consistent with t(28) = 2.0, even though the p-value recalculated from exactly 2.0, .055, is not below .05. If rounding were ignored, perfectly correct results would be flagged as errors whenever the test statistic was reported with few decimals.
It is not widely understood that statcheck accounts for this. For each result, it does not calculate a single p-value, but the range of p-values that could have produced the reported result after rounding. It takes the test statistic as reported, adds and subtracts half a unit of its last reported decimal (± 0.05 for t = 2.0, ± 0.005 for t = 1.46), and calculates the p-value at each end of this range. statcheck returns these bounds as computed_p_lower_bound and computed_p_upper_bound. Then, if the reported p-value is exact (e.g., p = .03), statcheck rounds the two bounds to the same number of decimals as the reported p-value and checks whether the reported value lies between them. If the reported p-value is an inequality (e.g., p &lt; .05), it checks whether any p-value in the range satisfies it. Only if the reported p-value is incompatible with the whole range is the result flagged.
The plot below shows how this works. Type a result in APA style into the box, or choose one of the examples. The upper panel shows the p-value as a function of the test statistic, on a logarithmic scale so that very small p-values can be seen. The blue dot is the recalculated p-value, and the orange line is the reported one; for a reported inequality, the shaded region shows the p-values it allows. The orange dot shows the test statistic that the reported p-value would require. The lower panel zooms in on the reported test statistic. Its vertical blue band shows the values of the test statistic that round to the reported value, and its horizontal blue band shows the range of p-values that these produce. The first button switches between two-sided and one-sided tests, and the second turns the accommodation of rounding on and off, so that you can see which results would be flagged without it. The classification follows statcheck’s rules, with α = .05.

