10.3 Recalculating descriptive statistics

10.3.1 Between overall sample and subgroups
Many articles report descriptive statistics for the whole sample and for subgroups of it, such as by gender, site, or condition, often in the same table. The whole sample’s N, mean, and SD are determined exactly by the subgroups’ ns, means, and SDs, so the whole-sample values can be recalculated from the subgroups’ values and compared with the reported ones.






NoteThe maths: combining subgroups into a whole sample





Cochrane Handbook’s formula for combining groups (Table 6.5.a in the current version). Note, not “pooled SD” since that term normally refers to the within-group-only SD used in t-tests and Cohen’s d, which ignores the mean differences.
Two groups:
\[
N = n_1 + n_2
\]
\[
M = \frac{n_1 M_1 + n_2 M_2}{N}
\]
\[
SD = \sqrt{\frac{(n_1-1)SD_1^2 + (n_2-1)SD_2^2 + \frac{n_1 n_2}{N}(M_1 - M_2)^2}{N-1}}
\]
k groups:
\[
SD^2 = \frac{\sum_i (n_i - 1) SD_i^2 + \sum_i n_i (M_i - M)^2}{N - 1}
\]



As with p-values (Section 10.1.1.3), the comparison has to allow for rounding. A subgroup mean reported as 4.58 could have been anything from 4.575 to 4.585, so the recalculated whole-sample mean is not a single value but a range, and the reported whole-sample mean is consistent with the subgroups if the range of values that round to it overlaps the recalculated range. Sample sizes are the exception. They are counts, not rounded values, so the reported N must equal the sum of the subgroups’ ns exactly.
When the reported N is larger than the sum of the subgroups’ ns, the most innocent explanation is that some participants are missing from the breakdown: participants who did not report their gender, for example, or a small category that was left out of the table. If a single subgroup is missing, the same formulas can be rearranged to find the n, mean, and SD it would need to have for the reported whole-sample values to be correct.






NoteThe maths: the subgroup that would be missing





Writing \(n_\star\), \(M_\star\), and \(SD_\star\) for the missing subgroup’s n, mean, and SD, and \(n_i\), \(M_i\), and \(SD_i\) for those of the reported subgroups:
\[
n_\star = N - \sum_i n_i
\]
\[
M_\star = \frac{N M - \sum_i n_i M_i}{n_\star}
\]
\[
SD_\star^2 = \frac{(N - 1) SD^2 - SS_i - n_\star (M_\star - M)^2}{n_\star - 1}
\]
where \(SS_i = \sum_i (n_i - 1) SD_i^2 + \sum_i n_i (M_i - M)^2\) is the part of the whole sample’s sum of squares that belongs to the reported subgroups.
On a scale from min to max, the largest SD that a group of \(n_\star\) with mean \(M_\star\) can have is the Bhatia–Davis bound (Bhatia & Davis, 2000):
\[
SD_\star \leq \sqrt{\frac{n_\star}{n_\star - 1}(M_\star - min)(max - M_\star)}
\]



These implied values can then be checked for whether they are possible at all. The implied variance can be negative, which no real group can have. The implied mean can fall outside the range of the scale. And the implied SD can be larger than any group of that size and mean could have on a bounded scale, a limit known as the Bhatia–Davis bound , Section 12.1.2. These are only the loosest bounds, and they are the only ones that the calculator below checks. If the scale is scored in whole numbers, the bounds are tighter: a missing subgroup of 6 can only have a mean that is a multiple of 1/6 (Section 11.2), and for each such mean its SD has a ceiling below the Bhatia–Davis bound and, unless the mean is itself a whole number, a floor above zero. The bounds methods in the {strait} package check these (Hussey, 2026). If the implied subgroup is impossible, a missing subgroup cannot explain the discrepancy. If it is possible, the explanation is plausible but not confirmed: the article may say elsewhere how many participants were left out of the breakdown, and why, which can be compared with the implied n.

10.3.1.1 Whole sample and subgroups calculator
The calculator below runs the recalc_total_from_subgroups() and recalc_missing_subgroup() functions from the {recalc} R package in your browser, using webR in the same way as the statcheck app (Section 10.1.1.1). Enter each subgroup’s n, mean, and SD, one subgroup per row. The means and SDs can be left empty if the article does not report them, in which case only the N is checked. You can paste a block of cells copied from a spreadsheet or a table, as in the SMD calculator (Section 10.2.1.2). Then, optionally, enter the whole-sample values that the article reports, and the minimum and maximum possible scores on the scale. Without reported whole-sample values, the calculator shows what they should be, given the subgroups. With them, it checks whether the reported values are consistent with the subgroups, and if the reported N is larger than the sum of the subgroups’ ns, it works out what a single unreported subgroup would need to be. The scale’s minimum and maximum are only used for this last step. The calculator handles up to 6 subgroups with SDs, and up to 12 without them, because the number of combinations of rounded values that it checks doubles with every value.
By default, the number of decimal places of the means and of the SDs is taken from the values as you typed them, so type 4.70 rather than 4.7 if the article reported two decimals. If values of the same kind were typed with different numbers of decimals, the fewest is used, which allows for the most rounding. Sample sizes are always taken as exact. {recalc} is not available as a webR package, so the calculator downloads the package’s R code from GitHub, at the version shown under the results. The starting values are simulated: a well-being scale scored from 1 to 7, reported for women and men, where 6 gender-diverse participants are included in the whole-sample values but not in the breakdown.

viewof sgInput = {
  const fields = [["label", "Subgroup"], ["n", "n"], ["m", "Mean"], ["sd", "SD"]]
  const starting = [["Women", "71", "4.58", "1.45"], ["Men", "62", "4.03", "1.19"]]
  const startingOverall = ["139", "4.30", "1.35"]
  const field = (label, value = "", numeric = true) =&gt; {
    const input = html`&lt;input type="text" class="form-control form-control-sm" inputmode="${numeric ? "decimal" : "text"}" aria-label="${label}"&gt;`
    input.value = value
    return input
  }
  const body = html`&lt;tbody&gt;&lt;/tbody&gt;`
  const addRow = (values = []) =&gt; {
    const tr = html`&lt;tr&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;`
    tr.inputs = fields.map(([key, label], j) =&gt; {
      const input = field(label, values[j] ?? "", j &gt; 0)
      input.onpaste = e =&gt; paste(e, tr, j)
      return input
    })
    const remove = html`&lt;button class="btn btn-sm smd-remove" title="Remove this subgroup" aria-label="Remove this subgroup"&gt;×&lt;/button&gt;`
    remove.onclick = () =&gt; { tr.remove(); if (body.children.length === 0) addRow() }
    tr.append(...tr.inputs.map(input =&gt; html`&lt;td&gt;${input}&lt;/td&gt;`), html`&lt;td&gt;${remove}&lt;/td&gt;`)
    body.append(tr)
    return tr
  }
  // a block of cells copied from a spreadsheet (tab-separated) or typed as comma-separated values fills the grid
  // from the cell it was pasted into, adding rows as needed. a single value is pasted as normal
  const paste = (e, tr, j) =&gt; {
    const text = e.clipboardData.getData("text").replace(/\r/g, "").replace(/\n+$/, "")
    if (!/[\t\n,]/.test(text)) return
    e.preventDefault()
    let row = tr
    for (const line of text.split("\n")) {
      const cells = line.split(line.includes("\t") ? "\t" : ",")
      cells.forEach((cell, k) =&gt; { if (j + k &lt; fields.length) row.inputs[j + k].value = cell.trim() })
      row = row.nextElementSibling ?? addRow()
    }
    // a trailing empty row that was only added for the next line is removed again
    if (row !== tr && row.inputs.every(input =&gt; input.value === "") && row.previousElementSibling) row.remove()
  }
  starting.forEach(values =&gt; addRow(values))
  const overall = ["Whole-sample N", "Whole-sample mean", "Whole-sample SD"].map((label, j) =&gt; field(label, startingOverall[j]))
  const scaleMin = field("Scale minimum", "1"), scaleMax = field("Scale maximum", "7")
  const digitsSelect = label =&gt; html`&lt;select class="form-select form-select-sm" aria-label="${label}"&gt;
    &lt;option value="auto"&gt;As typed&lt;/option&gt;${[0, 1, 2, 3, 4].map(d =&gt; html`&lt;option value="${d}"&gt;${d}&lt;/option&gt;`)}
  &lt;/select&gt;`
  const meanDigits = digitsSelect("Decimal places of the means"), sdDigits = digitsSelect("Decimal places of the SDs")
  const rounding = html`&lt;select class="form-select form-select-sm" aria-label="How the reported values were shortened"&gt;
    &lt;option value="either"&gt;Rounded&lt;/option&gt;
    &lt;option value="truncate"&gt;Truncated&lt;/option&gt;
  &lt;/select&gt;`
  const add = html`&lt;button class="btn btn-quarto"&gt;Add subgroup&lt;/button&gt;`
  const clear = html`&lt;button class="btn btn-quarto"&gt;Clear all&lt;/button&gt;`
  const run = html`&lt;button class="btn btn-quarto"&gt;Check&lt;/button&gt;`
  add.onclick = () =&gt; addRow().inputs[0].focus()
  clear.onclick = () =&gt; {
    body.replaceChildren()
    for (const input of [...overall, scaleMin, scaleMax]) input.value = ""
    addRow().inputs[0].focus()
  }
  const view = html`&lt;div&gt;
    &lt;div class="table-responsive"&gt;&lt;table class="table table-sm smd-grid sg-grid"&gt;
      &lt;thead&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Subgroup&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;&lt;em&gt;n&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;&lt;em&gt;M&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;&lt;em&gt;SD&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/thead&gt;
      ${body}
      &lt;tfoot&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class="sg-overall-label"&gt;Whole sample, as reported (optional)&lt;/td&gt;${overall.map(input =&gt; html`&lt;td&gt;${input}&lt;/td&gt;`)}&lt;td&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/tfoot&gt;
    &lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class="sg-controls"&gt;
      &lt;label&gt;&lt;span&gt;Scale minimum (optional)&lt;/span&gt;${scaleMin}&lt;/label&gt;
      &lt;label&gt;&lt;span&gt;Scale maximum (optional)&lt;/span&gt;${scaleMax}&lt;/label&gt;
      &lt;label&gt;&lt;span&gt;Decimals of the means&lt;/span&gt;${meanDigits}&lt;/label&gt;
      &lt;label&gt;&lt;span&gt;Decimals of the &lt;em&gt;SD&lt;/em&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;${sdDigits}&lt;/label&gt;
      &lt;label&gt;&lt;span&gt;Reported values were&lt;/span&gt;${rounding}&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class="sb-buttons"&gt;${add}${clear}${run}&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;`
  // the value only changes when the check button is clicked
  view.value = {runs: 0}
  run.onclick = () =&gt; {
    const rows = [...body.children].map(tr =&gt; Object.fromEntries(fields.map(([key], j) =&gt; [key, tr.inputs[j].value.trim()])))
    view.value = {
      runs: view.value.runs + 1, rows,
      overall: {n: overall[0].value.trim(), m: overall[1].value.trim(), sd: overall[2].value.trim()},
      scaleMin: scaleMin.value.trim(), scaleMax: scaleMax.value.trim(),
      meanDigits: meanDigits.value, sdDigits: sdDigits.value, rounding: rounding.value
    }
    view.dispatchEvent(new Event("input", {bubbles: true}))
  }
  return view
}







