The mean and SD are also a complete description of a normal distribution. If you know that data are normally distributed and you know their mean and SD, you know everything about the distribution: it is symmetric, it has no minimum or maximum, and the proportion of scores in any interval follows from the mean and SD alone. This is why reporting a mean and an SD feels like a full summary of a sample. It is, but only when the data are something like normal.


12.1.2 Mean and SD when data are bounded
Scales in psychology have a minimum and a maximum. A natural distribution for data that lie between two bounds is the beta distribution. It is defined on the interval from 0 to 1, and can be stretched to any other range: a score on a 1–7 scale is a beta-distributed value multiplied by 6 and shifted up by 1. The left plot below is a single-sample version of the plot in Section 8.1: one beta distribution on a 1–7 scale, whose parameters are calculated from the mean and SD you choose with the sliders. The grey areas are outside the scale. The scale starts as 1–7, and you can change its logical minimum and maximum in the two boxes above the sliders. The right plot is the same space of means and SDs as above, and you can again click or drag anywhere in it to set the mean and SD.

